WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.736
-Our first guest tonight is
an Emmy Award-Winning director,

00:00:03.736 --> 00:00:05.505
writer, and actor you know
from his work

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:08.041
on the fantastic limited series
"Escape at Dannemora"

00:00:08.041 --> 00:00:10.810
and films such as "Zoolander"
and "Meet the Parents."

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:13.078
He both executive-produced
and directed "Severance."

00:00:13.078 --> 00:00:16.048
You can catch new episodes
on Apple TV+ every Friday,

00:00:16.048 --> 00:00:18.718
with the season finale
available on April 8th.

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:21.020
Please welcome back to the show
our friend Ben Stiller,

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.288
everybody!

00:00:22.288 --> 00:00:25.291
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:25.291 --> 00:00:32.064
♪♪

00:00:32.064 --> 00:00:38.872
♪♪

00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:40.773
Welcome back!
I'm so happy you're here.

00:00:40.773 --> 00:00:44.576
-I'm so happy to be.
I feel like I'm in the show.

00:00:44.576 --> 00:00:45.878
I'm a fan of the show.

00:00:45.878 --> 00:00:47.713
So when you're on something
that you're a fan of,

00:00:47.713 --> 00:00:49.582
it's kind of, I feel like
I'm in the television.

00:00:49.582 --> 00:00:51.084
-Oh, well, that's --
I'm glad we can

00:00:51.084 --> 00:00:53.153
make that very eerie thing
happen for you.

00:00:53.153 --> 00:00:55.454
-I love --
I've told you this before,

00:00:55.454 --> 00:00:56.623
but I love the show

00:00:56.623 --> 00:00:58.158
and I love what you do
at "A Closer Look."

00:00:58.158 --> 00:01:00.059
It's exciting to see
"A Closer Look" live.

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:02.028
-[ Laughing ]
Thank you very much.

00:01:02.028 --> 00:01:03.896
-Or a distant look.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:03.896 --> 00:01:05.197
-[ Laughs ]
-And I just think --

00:01:05.197 --> 00:01:07.933
And you're one of our
unsung great impressionists.

00:01:07.933 --> 00:01:09.202
-Thank you very much.
-Right?

00:01:09.202 --> 00:01:10.603
isn't he one of
the best impressionists?

00:01:10.603 --> 00:01:13.539
-I'm blooming late
as an impressionist.

00:01:13.539 --> 00:01:15.575
-But they're really good
and really specific.

00:01:15.575 --> 00:01:18.377
The Pacino -- Just now,
the Pacino, De Niro.

00:01:18.377 --> 00:01:20.547
-I don't want to be --
I'd love to be humble,

00:01:20.547 --> 00:01:21.613
but I'm really good at them.

00:01:21.613 --> 00:01:22.915
-Yeah, no,
you are really good.

00:01:22.915 --> 00:01:24.650
[ Laughter ]
You're like Byner level.

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:27.219
-[ Laughs ]
That's so kind.

00:01:27.219 --> 00:01:30.155
Hey, so, you -- Obviously,
you grew up in show business.

00:01:30.155 --> 00:01:31.624
-Yes.
-Your parents were

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:33.926
two fantastic comedians,
Jerry Stiller and Anne Meara.

00:01:33.926 --> 00:01:35.528
Grew up in New York,
spent a lot of time in L.A.

00:01:35.528 --> 00:01:36.695
-Mm-hmm.
-And obviously,

00:01:36.695 --> 00:01:38.298
you fell in love
through your parents,

00:01:38.298 --> 00:01:40.566
but also as a viewer
of movies and television.

00:01:40.566 --> 00:01:43.236
Do you remember, like, early,
going to the movies,

00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:46.406
early on in your life?
-I've always loved movies.

00:01:46.406 --> 00:01:48.140
You know, grew up in the '70s,
so it was

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:51.578
a great era for movies.
And, yeah, my parents were

00:01:51.578 --> 00:01:54.613
always working in Los Angeles
a lot.

00:01:54.613 --> 00:01:56.583
We lived in New York,
so my sister and I

00:01:56.583 --> 00:01:58.217
would go out with them sometimes
in the summer

00:01:58.217 --> 00:01:59.718
when they would work.

00:01:59.718 --> 00:02:04.090
And one time, they were staying
at the Sunset Marquis in L.A.,

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:05.791
which is this sort of
like cool, hip hotel.

00:02:05.791 --> 00:02:07.593
It was probably like 1974.

00:02:07.593 --> 00:02:09.429
And they went off
to do a game show.

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:12.465
Might have been
"Hollywood Squares"

00:02:12.465 --> 00:02:13.999
or "Tattletales"
or something like that.

00:02:13.999 --> 00:02:17.569
And my sister and I got the girl
who was babysitting us,

00:02:17.569 --> 00:02:19.939
this 16-year-old -- apparently,
she was an actress, too.

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:21.407
I don't remember.

00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:24.710
But she took us
to see "The Exorcist."

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:26.946
-Okay. How old are you?

00:02:26.946 --> 00:02:30.216
-I am nine.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:30.216 --> 00:02:31.784
I was really excited

00:02:31.784 --> 00:02:33.986
because my parents
didn't know we went.

00:02:33.986 --> 00:02:36.288
We took a cab down to, I think
was, like, the Chinese Theatre

00:02:36.288 --> 00:02:37.723
or something.
And we went in,

00:02:37.723 --> 00:02:41.527
and we saw one of the most
horrifying movies ever made,

00:02:41.527 --> 00:02:45.231
that had an incredibly --
I mean, literally,

00:02:45.231 --> 00:02:48.500
I was so affected by it,
I couldn't speak.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:50.669
We went back to the hotel.

00:02:50.669 --> 00:02:54.173
My parents came back from work,
and I was like this.

00:02:54.173 --> 00:02:55.641
[ Laughter ]

00:02:55.641 --> 00:02:57.576
Insisted on sleeping with them
in the bed for the whole week.

00:02:57.576 --> 00:02:59.812
And of course, we never
told them what happened.

00:02:59.812 --> 00:03:01.180
-Oh, God!
-Yeah.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:03.082
-They probably thought something
far worse happened to you.

00:03:03.082 --> 00:03:04.250
[ Laughter ]

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.286
-Yeah, right, yeah.

00:03:06.286 --> 00:03:08.388
And I mean, it was Hollywood.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:08.388 --> 00:03:10.723
But it was an amazing movie.

00:03:10.723 --> 00:03:11.924
But, yeah, I'll always
remember that.

00:03:11.924 --> 00:03:13.092
-Probably a little too early.

00:03:13.092 --> 00:03:14.827
It's so funny,
as having kids now,

00:03:14.827 --> 00:03:18.097
that realizing that threshold of
when it's the right time

00:03:18.097 --> 00:03:20.066
to show a movie,
because in your head,

00:03:20.066 --> 00:03:21.600
you remember you were fine
when you saw it,

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:23.268
but they really can
do a number on you.

00:03:23.268 --> 00:03:25.571
-Yes, for sure.
And also the movies back then

00:03:25.571 --> 00:03:27.974
that were acceptable for kids
to see at that age,

00:03:27.974 --> 00:03:29.441
even though we were probably
a little young

00:03:29.441 --> 00:03:31.777
for "Exorcist," but, you know,
all the action movies,

00:03:31.777 --> 00:03:34.146
even, you know, people are
getting killed all the time.

00:03:34.146 --> 00:03:35.281
-[ Chuckling ] Yeah.

00:03:35.281 --> 00:03:36.516
-It's just like
a whole different world.

00:03:36.516 --> 00:03:37.916
People smoking cigarettes,
killing people.

00:03:37.916 --> 00:03:40.286
-Yeah, yeah, it's true.
You start these movies

00:03:40.286 --> 00:03:41.553
and you're like,
"Now, this is the good guy.

00:03:41.553 --> 00:03:43.890
Everything he does, you're
not allowed to ever do."

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:46.959
-Right, right.
-I know this about you

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:48.961
because, obviously, we know you
as an actor and a comedian.

00:03:48.961 --> 00:03:50.930
It's been so exciting,

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:52.465
both with this show
and "Dannemora,"

00:03:52.465 --> 00:03:54.167
you did want
to be a director first,

00:03:54.167 --> 00:03:55.768
more than anything.
-Yeah, I always wanted

00:03:55.768 --> 00:03:56.970
to be a director
since I was like

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:58.571
probably 9 or 10 years old,

00:03:58.571 --> 00:04:00.606
and my parents were always,
you know, actors.

00:04:00.606 --> 00:04:02.842
but I always thought,
"Oh, I want to direct."

00:04:02.842 --> 00:04:04.777
And I would, you know,
get all the magazines

00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:06.479
like, you know,
"American Cinematographer."

00:04:06.479 --> 00:04:08.314
And I was fascinated
with the behind the scenes,

00:04:08.314 --> 00:04:10.883
and I was making Super 8 movies
as a kid.

00:04:10.883 --> 00:04:12.518
-So, you have a Super 8 camera,

00:04:12.518 --> 00:04:14.153
and then what kind of
movies are you making?

00:04:14.153 --> 00:04:16.689
-I was making little
murder movies in Riverside Park.

00:04:16.689 --> 00:04:18.724
-Yeah, okay.
-Yeah, it was, you know, like,

00:04:18.724 --> 00:04:20.560
it was the time that
"Death Wish" was, you know --

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.429
-Yeah, and probably
that era of New York,

00:04:23.429 --> 00:04:24.797
you might actually
catch a murder

00:04:24.797 --> 00:04:26.331
if you were out there
just shooting.

00:04:26.331 --> 00:04:28.868
-Possibly.
The Zapruder film image.

00:04:28.868 --> 00:04:30.736
-Who were your cast
in those early movies?

00:04:30.736 --> 00:04:32.839
-It was usually friends.

00:04:32.839 --> 00:04:35.241
Or once I did
"The Tell-Tale Heart."

00:04:35.241 --> 00:04:37.310
You know that story?
-The Poe?

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:40.179
-The Edgar Allan Poe story.
It's about a man who lives --

00:04:40.179 --> 00:04:42.581
It's a weird story.
It's a guy who lives with a man,

00:04:42.581 --> 00:04:46.886
just he lives with him,
and the man has an evil eye.

00:04:46.886 --> 00:04:48.287
The guy believes
he has an evil eye.

00:04:48.287 --> 00:04:50.556
So every night, he sleeps
with his eye open,

00:04:50.556 --> 00:04:52.358
and the guy goes in
to check on him,

00:04:52.358 --> 00:04:54.260
and he's convinced the evil eye
is staring at him.

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:55.762
So he decides
he has to kill the guy.

00:04:55.762 --> 00:04:57.363
That's the story.
-Yeah.

00:04:57.363 --> 00:04:59.899
-So, we made the movie,

00:04:59.899 --> 00:05:02.034
and I cast my dad
as the man with the evil eye.

00:05:02.034 --> 00:05:03.769
-Uh-huh.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:03.769 --> 00:05:05.638
-And we shot
at my friend's house,

00:05:05.638 --> 00:05:06.772
and my dad was awesome.

00:05:06.772 --> 00:05:08.974
He totally supported
all the moviemaking.

00:05:08.974 --> 00:05:12.845
And you know, he got, like,
an old-timey kind of,

00:05:12.845 --> 00:05:14.547
you know, nightgown
that we rented.

00:05:14.547 --> 00:05:17.016
And then he was in the bed and
he'd, like, hold his eye open

00:05:17.016 --> 00:05:18.584
and we'd zoom in on his eye.

00:05:18.584 --> 00:05:20.285
And then my friend
had to chop him up.

00:05:20.285 --> 00:05:22.287
And so we put him
in the bathtub,

00:05:22.287 --> 00:05:24.991
and he put his, you know,
his leg over the bathtub edge,

00:05:24.991 --> 00:05:26.926
and we pretended we were
chopping his leg off.

00:05:26.926 --> 00:05:28.094
[ Laughter ]

00:05:28.094 --> 00:05:30.129
-I don't know, I feel
like you were maybe

00:05:30.129 --> 00:05:32.532
too old for "The Exorcist"
when you saw it.

00:05:32.532 --> 00:05:34.100
[ Laughter ]

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:36.068
-It might have affected
that movie, yeah.

00:05:36.068 --> 00:05:37.736
I also did "King Kong."

00:05:37.736 --> 00:05:40.439
That was before, like, remember
the Jeff Bridges "King Kong"?

00:05:40.439 --> 00:05:42.274
-Yes, of course.
-Yeah, I got obsessed with that,

00:05:42.274 --> 00:05:45.077
and I decided I was gonna
build the King Kong hand.

00:05:45.077 --> 00:05:46.545
That's how they did
the special effects,

00:05:46.545 --> 00:05:47.579
where she would get in the hand

00:05:47.579 --> 00:05:48.748
and they'd have
a rear projection.

00:05:48.748 --> 00:05:50.750
And so I got as far
as getting the lumber

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:52.684
and getting, like,
a piece of plywood

00:05:52.684 --> 00:05:55.387
and 2x4s and hinges.
And I made the hand.

00:05:55.387 --> 00:05:57.056
-Really?
-Just, like, the armature.

00:05:57.056 --> 00:05:58.658
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:58.658 --> 00:06:00.960
-And then I sort of
ran out of gas.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:02.361
[ Laughter ]

00:06:02.361 --> 00:06:05.231
And it just was in my room
for like two years,

00:06:05.231 --> 00:06:08.500
this weird wooden hand.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:11.637
-So, I think I recognized you
years ago,

00:06:11.637 --> 00:06:13.439
'cause I saw this movie,
not when it came out,

00:06:13.439 --> 00:06:15.408
but years later.
You were in a Spielberg film

00:06:15.408 --> 00:06:18.511
as a young man, as an actor?
-Mm-hmm, "Empire of the Sun."

00:06:18.511 --> 00:06:20.279
It was one of
the first jobs I had,

00:06:20.279 --> 00:06:22.315
and we played prisoners of war.

00:06:22.315 --> 00:06:23.916
It was Christian Bale's
first movie,

00:06:23.916 --> 00:06:26.486
and it's based on
a J.G. Ballard novel.

00:06:26.486 --> 00:06:28.553
So we were in
a prisoner-of-war camp

00:06:28.553 --> 00:06:30.122
that was supposed to be
in China,

00:06:30.122 --> 00:06:31.390
but we shot in Spain,

00:06:31.390 --> 00:06:33.459
and I was just, like,
almost an extra.

00:06:33.459 --> 00:06:35.861
I had like two lines.
And it was really exciting

00:06:35.861 --> 00:06:37.596
'cause I was
a huge Spielberg fan.

00:06:37.596 --> 00:06:39.498
And he'd come to see
a play I was in,

00:06:39.498 --> 00:06:42.201
and he basically cast me
in the movie.

00:06:42.201 --> 00:06:44.136
He said, "Hey, you know, I think
you'd be great for this part.

00:06:44.136 --> 00:06:45.437
Do you think
you could lose some weight,

00:06:45.437 --> 00:06:46.806
because you're supposed
to be in a prison camp?"

00:06:46.806 --> 00:06:49.375
So I showed up at the set.
I had lost about 28 pounds.

00:06:49.375 --> 00:06:51.344
And he looked at me
and he said, "Are you okay?"

00:06:51.344 --> 00:06:52.412
[ Laughter ]

00:06:52.412 --> 00:06:53.512
And I said, "You told me
to lose weight.

00:06:53.512 --> 00:06:55.648
You're Steven Spielberg."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:55.648 --> 00:06:59.151
But I did, one time,
I had an awful moment

00:06:59.151 --> 00:07:02.588
where there was, like,
a really long Steadicam shot,

00:07:02.588 --> 00:07:03.756
you know, where the camera
is just, like,

00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:05.191
following Christian bale out.

00:07:05.191 --> 00:07:06.759
He's, like, coming out
from the reeds.

00:07:06.759 --> 00:07:09.194
He was, like, hunting a quail
or a pheasant or something.

00:07:09.194 --> 00:07:10.963
And he comes back in
through the barracks

00:07:10.963 --> 00:07:12.832
and finally gets to us.

00:07:12.832 --> 00:07:14.167
I'm next to John Malkovich.

00:07:14.167 --> 00:07:15.367
I have one line
in the whole movie,

00:07:15.367 --> 00:07:16.902
and the camera gets to me.

00:07:16.902 --> 00:07:18.671
it's after like three or four
minutes of this shot.

00:07:18.671 --> 00:07:20.172
And I say my line.

00:07:20.172 --> 00:07:22.475
I'm supposed to say, "Hey, kid,
would you like a Hershey bar?"

00:07:22.475 --> 00:07:24.043
And I screwed it up, and I said,

00:07:24.043 --> 00:07:25.511
"Hey, kid, would you like
a chocolate bar?"

00:07:25.511 --> 00:07:28.080
And then I went, "Oh, damn,
I'm sorry, cut."

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:30.382
And there's silence.

00:07:30.382 --> 00:07:34.086
And then I just hear, from out
where the monitors are, "What?"

00:07:34.086 --> 00:07:35.320
[ Laughter ]

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:37.556
I was like, "I'm sorry.
I screwed up my line."

00:07:37.556 --> 00:07:41.360
And then I hear Steven Spielberg
say, "You never yell 'cut'!"

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:42.428
[ Laughter ]

00:07:42.428 --> 00:07:46.332
And of course, my hero, my idol.

00:07:46.332 --> 00:07:48.734
I'm realizing I just did the
most embarrassing thing ever.

00:07:48.734 --> 00:07:51.137
So it worked out fine.
-Yeah, it worked out.

00:07:51.137 --> 00:07:52.604
I guess it really
speaks to the fact

00:07:52.604 --> 00:07:53.906
that "director first,"

00:07:53.906 --> 00:07:56.108
if you're willing to yell "cut"
on the set.

00:07:56.108 --> 00:07:57.343
-I couldn't help myself.

00:07:57.343 --> 00:07:58.444
-He probably took you aside
and he's like,

00:07:58.444 --> 00:08:00.712
"You should get into directing."
-Exactly.

00:08:00.712 --> 00:08:02.682
-I have so many questions
about "Severance."

00:08:02.682 --> 00:08:04.684
We'll be right back
with more from Ben Stiller.

